    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That's a great idea; adding a touch of genuine struggle makes it feel much less like a template. You want to convey that the decision was genuinely difficult, not just a simple pass/fail.\n\nHere is a revised version with a more personal touch:\n\n\"Dear [Candidate Name],\n\nThank you again for investing so much time in our interview process for the [Job Title] role. We were genuinely impressed with your qualifications, and honestly, the final selection was incredibly difficult! We had several outstanding candidates, and the decision came down to a very close call. While we have decided to move forward with someone else for this particular opening, please know how much we appreciated meeting you and learning about your journey. We truly hope our paths cross again in the future, and we wish you the very best of luck in finding a wonderful role.\"\n\n**Why this works:** Phrases like \"genuinely impressed\" and \"incredibly difficult\" inject human emotion. It frames the rejection as a tough choice among many good options, which is much kinder than suggesting they just weren't good enough.",
